  "title": "Alzheimer's disease predicted effector genes (Bellenguez et al., 2022)",
  "bodyHtml": "<p>This table displays results from the study:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>New insights into the genetic etiology of Alzheimer’s disease and related dementias.<br />\r\nBellenguez C, Küçükali F, Jansen IE, Kleineidam L, Moreno-Grau S, Amin S, Naj AC, Campos-Martin R, <i>et al.</i><br />\r\nNat Genet. 2022 Apr;54(4):412-436. doi: 10.1038/s41588-022-01024-z.<br />\r\nPMID: <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/35379992\" target=\"_blank\"> 35379992 </a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>In this study, a large GWAS meta-analysis identified 42 new genetic loci&nbsp;that were significantly associated with risk of Alzheimer’s disease (AD) and related dementias (ADD). Multiple evidence types were then considered to prioritize genes at each locus:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n\t<li>molecular QTLs from tissues, cell types and brain regions relevant to ADD</li>\r\n\t<li>colocalization of molecular QTLs with genetic association signals</li>\r\n\t<li>lead variant allele frequency, protein-altering effects and nearest protein-coding gene</li>\r\n\t<li>results from a genome-wide, high-content short interfering RNA screen for amyloid-β precursor protein (APP) metabolism (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27933404/\" target=\"_blank\">Chapuis <i>et al</i>., 2017</a>)</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Genes were assigned a score between 0 (weakest evidence) and 100 (strongest evidence). They were also ranked as tier 1 (greater likelihood of being the causal risk gene responsible for the ADD signal) or tier 2 (lower likelihood and the absence of a minimum level of evidence as a causal risk gene). Genes with the weakest evidence were not ranked.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Click the <strong>Research Method</strong> tab for more details.</p>\r\n",
